

## 1. Purpose

Given an incoming regulatory alert, decide — risk by risk — whether it bears on each of the bank's standing Library Risks.

The output is a set of Record → Library Risk links, scored and evidenced, replacing a manual mapping exercise. Two lane designs have been built and measured against the same golden dataset.

---

## 2. Architecture

### 2.1 The two lanes

| | **Lane A — routed** | **Lane B — direct** |
|---|---|---|
| Path | Alert → Regulation Summary (RSM) → Library Risk | Alert → Library Risk |
| Candidates | Library risks sitting under the summaries the alert was linked to | Every published library risk with text |
| Pairs judged | 2,409 | 18,560 |
| Model calls | ~60 | 480 |
| Upstream dependency | Yes — Alert→RSM run | None |

Lane B was built to answer one question: what was the route costing us? Everything else is held constant — same 160 alerts, same golden, same prompt, same model, same judge machinery, same `risk_text` construction. Only the candidate source changes.

### 2.2 Pipeline sections

Both lanes share a section structure:

| Section | Purpose |
|---|---|
| [0] | Imports, paths, gateway binding |
| [A] | Data load — the mapping extracts, risk register, alerts, golden |
| [A1] | Scope recovery (direct lane only) — pins the run to the same records as the routed lane |
| [A2] | Validation probe — run **before** building anything |
| [B1] | Config, model params, response schema |
| [B2] | Reachability — what the recall ceiling actually is |
| [C] | Candidate build / work plan + concurrent judge execution |
| [D] | Performance scorecard — **golden enters here, validation only** |
| [E] | Diagnostic — where the misses go |
| [F] | LLM-as-a-judge miss audit |
| [F1] | Honest recall + waterfall |
| [F0] | Head-to-head (direct lane only) |
| [G] | Performance pack — confusion matrix + documentation workbook |

### 2.3 Data sources

| Source | Grain | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| Mapping extract — summaries & taxonomy | One row per Regulation Summary | Funnel, RSM → regulation parentage |
| Mapping extract — summaries & library controls | RSM × L1 control × L2 control | **RSM → Library Risk parentage** (dedupe to the RSM–LR pair) |
| Risk register — US risk extract | Risk **instance** | Library risk text (collapse to one row per library risk) |
| Alert store | One row per record | Alert text |
| Golden dataset | Record → Regulation → Library Risk | **Validation only** — never used to build candidates |

### 2.4 Data gotchas worth knowing

- **The risk extract is keyed by risk *instance*, not library risk.** The library risk is carried as parent fields on every instance row. Collapse to one row per library risk, Published only, newest instance wins. Do **not** use the instance-level columns (title, description, risk_owner, inherent/residual risk, lifecycle comments) — they vary across instances of the same library risk.
- **Taxonomy, business and legal-entity lines are part of the risk's meaning**, not metadata. The one-line description alone is too thin to judge on. They are concatenated into `risk_text`.
- **Blank IDs arrive as `"-"`, `"NONE"`, `"NAN"`, `"NAT"`**, not as nulls. Filter them before any `nunique()` or join, or counts come out one too high.
- **Normalise every ID** (string / strip / upper) before joining, or joins silently miss on case and whitespace.
- The mapping extract is at RSM × L1-control × L2-control grain, so there are multiple rows per RSM. Dedupe to the (RSM, LR) pair.

