## 4. How performance is measured

### 4.1 The measurement trap

The golden is **hand-picked, non-exhaustive, and built at regulation level with no Regulation Summary column.** When a record touches a regulation it inherits every risk sitting under every summary of that regulation. The average regulation carries ~2.7 summaries.

Two consequences that govern how we are allowed to report:

1. A golden risk link is verified truth → **we can measure recall.**
2. A predicted link **not** in the golden is **not** a false positive — the golden is incomplete. We report these as **discoveries**. **Precision and F1 are therefore never computed.**

### 4.2 The recall definitions — and which to quote

| Definition | Numerator | Denominator | When to use |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Addressable (naïve) recall** | Caught | All addressable golden links (1,229) | **The only cross-lane comparable number.** Inflated denominator. |
| Recall vs golden as handed (judged pairs) | Caught | Golden links the model actually judged | Single-lane reporting only. Bases differ across lanes — see warning below. |
| **Text-derivable recall** | Caught | Caught + audit-derivable misses + insufficient | The number that describes model quality. Routed lane only. |
| Model-addressable recall | Caught | As above, less SME-only links | As above. |

> ⚠️ **Denominator warning.** "Recall vs judged pairs" uses 1,098 for the routed lane (131 pairs were never judged) and 1,229 for the direct lane (nothing was withheld). Putting 23.0% next to 22.2% reads as a regression when it is the opposite. **Compare lanes on the addressable row only.**

